## Session summary

**Step 5.2 of defect #1 is blocked on a newly-found critical defect that I measured on the rig: the mount-incarnation identifier is a hard-coded zero.**

### What happened

I started step 5.2 (`token v2: be64 resource, **bound to victim slot + incarnation**`) and went looking for the incarnation value to bind to. `struct mxfs_disklock_ctx.epoch` is written exactly once in the whole tree — `ctx->epoch = 0` in the constructor — and read everywhere else.

I measured it rather than trusting the grep. `dd` of the 64-slot heartbeat region off the live shared LUN (`/dev/mapper/mpatha`, `disklock_offset=67117056` from `chk_mxfs -v`, epoch at byte 24), decoded in python3 independently of the kernel module:

```
slot= 0 magic=0x4d584c4b flags=1 node=2204047808 fs_gen=1167495961 EPOCH=0
... 31 live records ...
distinct EPOCH values across all live HB records: [0]
```

### What that silently defeats

Seven predicate families, all in `dlm/disklock.c` — and they all *appear to pass* today:

- `hb_own_record()` — the sess78 own-slot CAS: a stale ACTIVE record from our **own previous incarnation** reads as our live record; the "different incarnation owns the slot" branch is unreachable.
- `desc.victim_epoch` — the durable recovery descriptor cannot name which incarnation it is recovering.
- `d->owner_epoch == ctx->epoch` (8 sites) collapses to `owner_node == local_node` — a **rejoined** node adopts its own prior incarnation's recovery lease. Same for `fence_prover_epoch` in the sess75/76 fence certificate.
- The idiom `if (victim_epoch && d->victim_epoch != victim_epoch)` is **skipped entirely** at ~8 recovery sites, because callers pass an epoch read from disk.

### The RULE-5 ruling (banked — it refuted my design)

My proposed `(wall_ms << 16) | rand16` was rejected: 16 bits of collision resistance under stuck/rolled-back clocks, which is exactly the VM-snapshot/rapid-reboot case. Use a **cryptographically random nonzero 64-bit** value, equality-only, never ordered, generated before the claim image is built, failing the claim closed if the RNG is unavailable. Land as one protocol transition under a `MXFS_PROTO_GEN` 2→3 bump; after it, zero means "no valid published incarnation" and is never a wildcard.

It also forced two things beyond the old 5.2 scope: token v2 needs an explicit **VALID/MIXED/INCOMPLETE/WRITE_AUTH** status (today `class==NONE` overloads six different conditions), and **last-writer-wins provenance is wrong** — differing contributions must merge to permanently MIXED, with provenance snapshotted alongside each CIL image.

### Landed

No code changes — the ruling arrived at the boundary. Both the measurement and the full ruling are in ccmemory (`sess83-MOUNT-INCARNATION-IS-CONSTANT-ZERO-MEASURED`, `sess83-GPT-ruling-incarnation-and-token-v2`), and the ledger now carries `D-MOUNT-INCARNATION-CONSTANT-ZERO` (critical) with a step-by-step `next`, plus defect #1's `next` repointed at it as a prerequisite. Board tally is now 19 OPEN / 12 critical.

**Criteria: NO.** 19 open defects, 12 critical, one of them newly proven. Not production ready.

